recommend check-in either -- **`%407` is CLOSED on a witness GREEN on metal, and the door this seat carried is answered by measurement rather than argument: a record-only notation should NOT default to Meter, because session logs moved 0 of 60 and already read right at Field.** What the 385-artifact reading found instead is one card reading one page of prose with two scored readings, and only one of them asking whether there was enough prose to read -- **207 sat under the register scan's own eight-sentence floor and 137 still took a scored Reach**, 17 of them carrying zero words and reading **reach=0**, the low point of the scale, for a page the card had read nothing of. The grade term now takes the same cited floor while the index door keeps the link term, so **137 readings rose, none fell**, 99 crossed to B, and all four index-door legs pass unchanged. **One reading worth your word, because it splits a door you already hold:** the card says Door's ceiling of 9 puts sampled sources below B and calls those grades honest -- of **115 of 163** sampled programs below B, **51 sat under this floor** with nothing measurable and **64** are genuinely scored at grades 9 to 23. The ceiling question is the second number's alone, and beside it Gauge's own settings table seats **witness headers** at Meter while the card grades every program head at Door. **One collision, resolved by the law rather than by argument:** a peer folded `%401` and booked `%405` and `%406` in the same hour, so my shelf was withdrawn and my unshared row renumbered to **`%407`** off the anointed spine -- the seventh recorded firing of exactly what `derived-spine` exists for. Their `%405` -- a pen keeping its own copy of what to stage -- then repaired **my** elder pen, which had gone quiet the same way; their row was written about two pens and mine was the third. **And the card leaves at 42 bytes free**, released rather than trimmed, which is the third lap running under 450. The release that bought it was the card's own directive applied to my own entry -- a landed round is one line pointing at its record -- and the live front still carries three closed-row narratives.
